<!-- MIDDLE: ARTICLE -->
<article class="post-body">

  <section id="s-vibe" data-code="vibe">
    <div class="section-no">01 — The Agentic Era</div>
    <h2>From Vibe Coding to Agentic Engineering</h2>
    <p>For the past few years, the dominant mode of AI development has been "vibe coding" — typing a prompt into a chat interface, evaluating the code by eye (or "vibes"), copy-pasting the output into an editor, and manually resolving errors. While fast for simple scripts, this workflow breaks down completely in real-world software engineering. Complex tasks require agents to navigate code bases, search directories, invoke terminal commands, parse build logs, and correct errors autonomously.</p>
    <p>Transitioning from vibe coding to <strong>agentic engineering</strong> changes the load on foundation models. Instead of single-turn completions, models must support long-horizon reasoning loops. They must process context windows spanning entire code repositories (up to 1M tokens in GLM-5.2) and generate code quickly enough to prevent rollout bottlenecks. Zhipu AI's GLM-5 family (744B–753B parameter Mixture-of-Experts backbone with ~40B active parameters) was redesigned from the ground up to solve these engineering bottlenecks.</p>
    
    <div class="vp">
      <div class="vp-head">
        <span class="vp-title">Agent vs Chatbot Execution Characteristics</span>
      </div>
      <div class="vp-body">
        <table class="comparison-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Metric</th>
              <th>Vibe Chatbot (e.g. GLM-4)</th>
              <th>Agentic Engineer (e.g. GLM-5.2)</th>
              <th>The Bottleneck</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Context Window</strong></td>
              <td>8K – 32K tokens</td>
              <td class="win">1,000,000 tokens (Native)</td>
              <td>Quadratic attention cost O(L²)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Average Turns</strong></td>
              <td>1 – 3 conversational turns</td>
              <td class="win">30+ tool-sandbox actions</td>
              <td>Catastrophic forgetting / state drift</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Decoding Load</strong></td>
              <td>Subjective natural text</td>
              <td class="win">Syntax-strict programming code</td>
              <td>High token generation latency</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>RL Strategy</strong></td>
              <td>RLHF (Human preference vibes)</td>
              <td class="win">Reasoning & Agent Environment RL</td>
              <td>Synchronous GPU idle bottlenecks</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="s-mtp" data-code="mtp">
    <div class="section-no">02 — Shared-Weight MTP</div>
    <h2>Speculative Decoding with Parameter-Shared MTP</h2>
    <p>Autoregressive generation generates tokens one by one, which is slow for large models. <strong>Speculative Decoding</strong> solves this by using a small "draft" model to guess future tokens (e.g. predicting 3–4 tokens ahead), and a large "target" model to verify them in parallel. However, training a separate draft model is complex. DeepSeek-V3 introduced Multi-Token Prediction (MTP), where additional layers are attached to the target model during pre-training to predict future tokens directly.</p>
    <p>But traditional MTP has a major flaw: predicting $n$ tokens requires $n$ separate MTP layers, which scales memory and KV cache overhead linearly. To save memory, DeepSeek-V3 trained with only 1 MTP layer but tried to predict 2 tokens at inference, resulting in a training-inference mismatch that degraded the acceptance rate (acceptance length 2.55). GLM-5 overcomes this by introducing <strong>Parameter-Shared MTP</strong>. It trains 3 MTP layers that share the exact same weights. This keeps memory overhead flat (equivalent to a single MTP layer) while increasing the speculative decoding acceptance length to <strong>2.76 tokens</strong> (an 8% to 20% speedup during inference).</p>
    
