  <section id="s-slime" data-code="slime">
    <div class="section-no">04 — Asynchronous slime RL</div>
    <h2>Decoupling Generation and Training: The slime Infrastructure</h2>
    <p>Training agentic models with Reinforcement Learning (RL) presents a system bottleneck. Agent environments (e.g. running code tests, executing search queries) are slow and synchronous. In naive RL pipelines, the expensive training GPUs sit idle (0% utilization) while waiting for the rollout engine to generate trajectories from the environment. This makes training extremely slow.</p>
    <p>To maximize GPU utilization, GLM-5 adopts the <strong>slime</strong> post-training framework. slime completely decouples the rollout engine (inference nodes) from the training engine (training nodes) via a central Multi-Task Rollout Orchestrator. The rollout engine runs asynchronously on cheaper hardware, generating trajectories and feeding them into a queue. The training engine pulls batches from this queue and updates weights continuously, keeping training GPUs at 100% utilization. To maintain stability during asynchronous training, slime implements a <strong>Token-in-Token-out (TITO)</strong> gateway to align tokenization steps and uses token-level clipping to control off-policy bias.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s-topk" data-code="topk">
    <div class="section-no">05 — Deterministic top-k</div>
    <h2>The DSA Stability Trick: Deterministic Top-k</h2>
    <p>During GLM-5's large-scale Reinforcement Learning training on the DSA architecture, researchers encountered severe stability issues. After only a few RL steps, the model's performance would drop drastically, accompanied by a sharp collapse in policy entropy. The culprit was the attention indexer's selection mechanism.</p>
    <p>To select the top-2048 keys, typical inference engines (like SGLang) use highly optimized CUDA or TileLang top-$k$ operators. Because these operators run parallel reductions across GPU threads, they are <strong>non-deterministic</strong> — the output indices can vary slightly across runs due to floating-point sorting order. While harmless during inference, this non-determinism introduces noise into the GRPO advantage estimates. The policy quickly collapses under this gradient variance. By replacing the custom CUDA kernels with PyTorch's slower but strictly <strong>deterministic</strong> <code>torch.topk</code> operator, the training stabilized, and the model resumed learning reasoning trajectories.</p>
    
